# Mandatory adversarial review

**Protocol version: 2.0. Blocking gate, not optional advice.**

## Why this exists

One authoring angle always leaves holes. Adversarial review is rubber-ducking with independent critics who look at the same plan or implementation from directions the author never took, so a hole invisible from the author's path shows up from someone else's.

Critics attack the artifact and explain **why it fails**. They do not praise, summarize, rubber-stamp, or improve prose. A new path working is not enough: the gate also has to prove that trigger cascades, shared dependencies, legacy flows, sibling RecordTypes, bulk transactions, async races, and existing callers still behave.

## Where this applies

**Required — Salesforce delivery:**

- Metadata under `force-app/` — Apex, LWC, Aura, Flow, OmniStudio, layouts, FlexiPages, fields, permission sets, validation rules, anything deployable.
- Org data mutations and any deploy.
- Org-wide retrieve / mirror runs and their audit docs.
- OmniStudio cache-bust and version-swap operations.
- The LLD and `changes/` docs that accompany any of the above.

**Not required unless the user asks:**

- Personal tooling under `scripts/`.
- The agent-guidance kit itself — rules, skills, doc templates, bootstrap.
- Notes and reference docs not tied to a delivery ticket.

**Anything not on the exempt list is in scope** — the lists above are not two halves of a closed set. In particular, configuration that governs what gets deployed, retrieved, or statically analysed is Salesforce delivery: `sfdx-project.json`, `.forceignore`, `manifest/`, `config/pmd-ruleset.xml`, and `config/schema/` regeneration all change what later work compiles against or silently skips.

**The carve-out is by artifact class, never by authorship.** Anything that deploys metadata, mutates org data, or runs DML is Salesforce delivery no matter which folder it lives in — a `scripts/` file that writes to the org is in scope. "I wrote this myself" and "it is only a small script" are not exemptions. When the user does ask for a review of kit work, use the agent-guidance profile below; that profile exists precisely so this carve-out never means "unreviewable."

**The exemption list is not self-amendable.** Edits to this rule, to the lists above, or to the Gate outcome sections of any template always require an agent-guidance Gate A — you may not widen your own exemption and then work inside it.

Planning-only work runs Gate A. Implemented work runs Gate A and Gate B. In-scope work that is genuinely tiny may answer concisely, and may record evidence-backed `N/A` for categories that cannot apply, but it does not skip the gate.

If work is inherited mid-implementation, freeze and fingerprint the inherited state, reconstruct current behavior, label which changes pre-existed, and run Gate A before any further edit.

## The two gates

Canonical order:

`intake → retrieve/current-state evidence → schema/domain inspection → draft plan/LLD → Gate A → edit/build/tests → Gate B → deploy or mutate → runtime verification → commit/handoff`

**Gate A — plan and design.** Runs once current behavior, the affected surface, cascading impact, and a draft design/LLD exist. Runs *before* the first source edit, or before delivering a planning-only conclusion.

**Gate B — implementation.** Runs once the implementation and tests are ready. Runs *before* the first real deploy, org DML, cache-bust, version swap, commit, or final handoff.

**Nothing in the review loop touches the live org.** Gate B runs against local artifacts plus validation-only output — `--dry-run`, `sf project deploy validate`, PMD, and synchronous test runs against the validation job. Every challenge round below therefore costs nothing but time; no back-and-forth can damage a live environment, because the deploy has not happened yet.

The single exception is Gate-A-reviewed observability setup — for example a narrowly scoped, time-bounded TraceFlag — when validation evidence cannot otherwise be captured. Record its exact payload, target user, expiry, and cleanup. It must never change business data or deployable metadata.

Only the root/orchestrating agent launches critics. Critics are read-only and MUST NOT spawn further critics.

## Scope contract — declare it before launching

Over-broad critique is the most common failure of this gate. In a shared repo, hundreds of components are touched by many teams; a critic given no boundary will report on all of them, and the useful findings drown. So every fan-out opens with an explicit contract:

- **Owned surface** — the exact list of paths this change edits. Enumerate them. No globs, no "and related files."
- **Blast radius** — everything already consuming what you edited: Apex callers and callees, shared helpers, sibling RecordTypes, trigger/flow cascades, OmniScripts and Integration Procedures that invoke the IP or DataRaptor you changed (and the IPs that invoke *those*), LWC/Aura components importing a changed Apex method, FlexiPages and layouts surfacing a changed field, permission sets granting it, and legacy paths still on the old route. This is what the change is accountable for even though it did not edit it. Enumerate it by searching for consumers, not by recalling them.
- **Not owned** — components that merely coexist in the repo, and other teams' code the change does not sit on.
- **Out of scope** — concerns that cannot apply to this artifact class, each with a one-line reason.
- **Profile** — follows the artifact, not preference: anything under `force-app/`, any deploy, and any org data mutation uses **Salesforce delivery**; the kit uses **agent-guidance**; a purpose-matched set of three is available only when neither applies. You may not downgrade an Apex change to "other" and drop the runtime and concurrency lenses.
- **Change kind** — `existing_modified`, `greenfield`, or `retrieve_mirror`. `existing_modified` raises the bar: regression on the shared path is the primary risk, not a secondary one.

A finding must land on the owned surface or its blast radius. "This unrelated component has a pre-existing bug" is dispositioned `Out of scope — outside declared ownership`, citing the contract.

**Both halves of that rule are binding.** A shared path the edited component sits on is *always* in scope, and pre-existing-ness is never a defence when this change makes the defect reachable, more frequent, or harder to detect. Do not use the contract to wave away a real regression on a component you touched; the contract exists to focus critics, not to shrink accountability.

## Evidence pack

Give every critic in a fan-out the same snapshot, and record it in the LLD (Gate A) or `changes/<slug>.md` (Gate B):

```bash
BASE=$(git merge-base HEAD <target-branch>)   # or the pre-work HEAD
git rev-parse HEAD
git diff --stat "$BASE" -- <explicit-path-1> <explicit-path-2>
git diff --name-only "$BASE"
git status -s
```

Record: base SHA, HEAD SHA, the explicit changed-path list, and the paths of any test / PMD / log output referenced.

**Gate A** additionally supplies requirements and ACs, current-behavior evidence, the affected surface, the dependency/cascade map, stated assumptions, the proposed design, rejected alternatives, and draft test scenarios.

**Gate B** additionally supplies the confirmed ACs, the approved design and its Gate A outcome, the complete diff, affected callers and callees, validation-job output, test results and coverage, static-analysis output, and every operation-bound Apex log ID. For Salesforce delivery, add a freshness recheck — the `LastModifiedDate` of each touched component, re-queried immediately before deploy, to prove nobody changed the org underneath you during the review.

Evidence that does not apply is recorded as `N/A` with a one-line reason. Salesforce logs and org freshness are legitimately `N/A` for guidance-only artifacts.

## Launch three independent critics in parallel

Launch all critics in a single parallel fan-out. Do not serialize them, and do not show one critic's findings to another before every critic has returned — independence is the entire mechanism, and sharing findings early produces groupthink instead of coverage.

### Salesforce delivery profile

1. **Runtime and limits** — trigger/flow/validation/OmniStudio/async chains; recursion; 1/200/max/mixed bulk; DML, SOQL, SOSL, or callouts in loops; governor limits; nulls, exceptions, rollback.
2. **Concurrency and data integrity** — stale reads, async ordering, locks and deadlocks, overlapping jobs, duplicates, retries and idempotency, transaction boundaries.
3. **Requirements, regression, and dependencies** — AC counterexamples, logical and state boundaries, callers/callees/shared metadata, permissions and FLS, RecordTypes, sibling and legacy paths, and the case where the new path succeeds while an old path silently regresses.

### Agent-guidance profile (rules, skills, templates, bootstrap kit)

1. **Rule and skill consistency** — contradictory mandates, wrong ordering, missing activation triggers or descriptions, duplicate ownership, impossible instructions, stale cross-references, and drift between a rule and its skill mirror.
2. **Template and workflow usability** — missing fields or checklists, unusable placeholders, numbering and anchors, evidence expectations, cleanup instructions, naming and slugs, and whether a future agent can follow the workflow without guessing.
3. **Portability and generation** — org-specific leakage into shared templates, token coverage for every machine- or org-specific value, template-to-generated parity, seed-once files that must never be overwritten, generated file placement and counts, and whether the bootstrapper reproduces the intended output in a fresh target.

For the agent-guidance profile the bootstrapper is a delivery mechanism, not the subject. Review whether it tokenizes, substitutes, places, and preserves the intended files. Do **not** redesign its locking, transaction model, crash durability, threat model, or large-scale performance unless the user asks or a concrete defect prevents correct output.

### Other artifact types

Derive exactly three independent lenses from the declared purpose. Keep one correctness/requirements lens and one regression/dependency lens; make the third domain-specific. Record why each standard Salesforce category is `N/A` rather than attacking systems the artifact does not touch.

Add a fourth specialist only when the declared in-scope surface warrants it. A fourth critic never replaces one of the three mandatory lenses.

## Critic prompt contract

Every prompt carries the scope contract verbatim, plus this instruction in substance:

> Attack and nitpick this artifact. Find concrete reasons it will fail or regress existing behavior. Do not praise it. Do not edit files. Return `BLOCK` for any Critical/High finding, `PASS_WITH_FINDINGS` when only Medium/Low findings remain, otherwise `PASS`.
>
> Stay inside the supplied scope contract and profile. A concern outside the declared surface is not a finding. Do not invent unrelated infrastructure requirements.
>
> One exception: **contract completeness is always in scope.** If `git diff --name-only` shows a path missing from the owned surface, or you find a consumer of an edited component missing from the blast radius, report that as a finding. An under-declared contract is the one way this gate can be quietly defeated, and you are the only party positioned to catch it.

Require each critic to return:

- Critic identity and assigned lens.
- The exact artifact revision reviewed (base SHA + path list) and any assumptions made.
- Overall `PASS`, `PASS_WITH_FINDINGS`, or `BLOCK`.
- Per finding: an ID and severity (`Critical`, `High`, `Medium`, `Low`).
- Concrete evidence — path and line, method, transaction chain, dependency, query, or metadata element.
- The triggering data shape, volume, timing, or interleaving.
- The failure consequence and which existing behavior it affects.
- A recommended fix, plus the regression/bulk/concurrency test that would prove it.

A vague concern with no falsifiable failure scenario is not a finding. A critic that fails, times out, or omits its assigned lens does not count toward the three.

## Disposition

The orchestrating agent deduplicates findings, **verifies each one against the actual source and evidence**, and records exactly one disposition:

- `Fixed` — design or implementation changed; cite the change and the test.
- `Rejected with evidence` — demonstrably a false positive; cite the proof.
- `Accepted risk` — Medium/Low only, with explicit user approval and rationale.
- `Deferred` — Medium/Low only, with a follow-up owner/ticket and containment.
- `N/A with evidence` — the category cannot apply; explain why.
- `Out of scope — outside declared ownership` — cite the scope contract.

Rules:

- `Critical` or `High` may only be `Fixed` or `Rejected with evidence`; they cannot be accepted, deferred, or dispositioned out of scope. A scope objection to a Critical or High goes to the user through the challenge loop, never to the contract.
- `Medium` findings require resolution or explicit user risk acceptance.
- `Low` findings remain recorded even when not fixed.
- Critic disagreement is unresolved risk, not a majority vote.
- Do not count three critics as three approvals; category coverage and evidence matter.

Aggregate gate verdict:

- `PASS` — all mandatory critics passed and no finding remains.
- `PASS_WITH_FINDINGS` — no critic is `BLOCK`; only resolved/accepted Medium or recorded Low findings remain.
- `BLOCK` — any critic blocks, any required lens or evidence is missing, or any Critical/High/unresolved Medium remains.

## The challenge loop

A critic is not an authority and its output is not a work order. Findings are hypotheses; the orchestrating agent's job is to test them. Two failures are equally serious: **applying a wrong finding**, and **dismissing a right one**.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    F[Critic returns finding] --> V{Verify against<br/>source and evidence}
    V -->|Verified true| FIX[Fixed: cite change + test]
    V -->|Verified false| REB[Write counter-evidence]
    V -->|Cannot verify| REB
    REB --> R{Rebuttal round<br/>to the SAME lens}
    R -->|Critic withdraws| REJ[Rejected with evidence:<br/>record both sides]
    R -->|Critic holds| C{Rounds used}
    C -->|Fewer than 3| REB
    C -->|3 reached| H[STOP. Ask the human.<br/>Mandatory. No self-resolve.]
    FIX --> N[New artifact revision:<br/>rerun all three lenses]
```

Binding rules:

1. **Never auto-apply.** Read the cited code, query, or log and confirm the failure is real before changing anything. Applying findings unverified is how a review makes an artifact worse.
2. **Never silently drop.** Rejecting a finding is not a private decision. Write the counter-evidence and send it back to that same lens as a rebuttal round, giving the critic the context it lacked. A finding that disappears without a recorded rebuttal is a bypass.
3. **Unverifiable means unresolved.** If you cannot prove a finding either way, it is not a false positive. It goes to a rebuttal round and, failing that, to the human.
4. **Three rounds, then a human — always.** A rebuttal round re-prompts the disagreeing lens with the added context. After three rounds on the same finding without resolution, **stop and ask the user.** Do not deploy, mutate, commit, or hand off. Do not convert a surviving Critical or High into `Accepted risk` or `Deferred` to escape the loop. Present both positions and the evidence; record the user's decision as the disposition, attributed to them.
5. **Convenience is never a reason.** Schedule pressure, effort, or a preference for the current design does not justify rejecting a valid finding.

## Re-review triggers

Distinguish the two cases; conflating them either wastes three critics on an unchanged artifact or, worse, ships a changed one on a stale verdict:

- **Rebuttal round** — the artifact is unchanged and only counter-evidence was added. Re-prompt just the disagreeing lens. The other two verdicts stand.
- **Revision** — any change to the artifact or its evidence. This starts a new revision and reruns **all three** mandatory critics in parallel against it.

Never combine verdicts from different revisions, and never accept a late result from a superseded one. Record the new revision and supersede — do not erase — the prior verdict.

**At most two full revisions per gate.** Critics are instructed to attack and forbidden to praise, so a third revision will usually surface a fourth round of findings; without a ceiling the gate has no termination guarantee and gets abandoned wholesale rather than run properly. If findings remain after the second revision, stop and take them to the user with the same escalation the challenge loop uses. Reaching the ceiling is a normal outcome, not a failure.

## Capability failures and anti-patterns

If parallel subagents are unavailable, report the gate as **unfulfilled and blocked**. Do not silently substitute a self-review or a single generic critic and call it compliance.

Forbidden:

- Running critics sequentially when parallel launch is available.
- Coaching critics toward approval, or giving later critics earlier findings.
- Treating "no findings" as proof without lens and category coverage.
- Majority-voting away a blocker.
- Applying a finding without verifying it first.
- Dropping a finding without a recorded rebuttal round.
- Self-resolving a finding that has already survived three rounds.
- Using the scope carve-out to skip review on something that writes to the org.
- Deploying, mutating org data, cache-busting, committing, or handing off before Gate B passes.
- Claiming in-scope work is exempt because it looks small.
